          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#TheArgument"} The “hole argument”
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Discussion"} Discussion
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            When the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            in the form of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            was developed at the beginning of the 20th century, the abstract notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            independently of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+charts"} coordinate charts
            was still unfamiliar. Theories like that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            were traditionally all formulated with respect to what today would be called a choice of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+patch"} coordinate patch
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"} Albert Einstein
            went through a phase in which what is now called the principle of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"} general covariance
            was only gradually understood.
          %p
            The “hole argument” or “hole
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paradox"} paradox
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#EinsteinGrossmann"} Einstein-Grossmann
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            on some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            – hence a field configuration of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : * \to X
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : X \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            around
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is entirely unrelated to the value of the
            %span.newWikiWord
              pullback
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/pullback+of+a+cotensor"} ?
            metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo *
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^* g
            at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            This was regarded as a source of puzzlement for a while – a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paradox"}> paradox
            ” – because if one thinks that the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is absolutely fixed (the locus of an “observer” in spacetime) and that two metrics related by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            as above are equivalent field configurations, it would seem that the field of gravity “at
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"} general covariance
            and indeed in (
            %a{:href => "#EinsteinGrossmann"}> Einstein-Grossmann
            ) the notion of general covariance was still rejected in view of this.
          %p
            But of course the value of the metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %em does
            coincide with that of
            = succeed "\u2026" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo *
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^* g
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Discussion"} Discussion
          %h2#TheArgument The “hole argument”
          %p The original argument was formulated more in detail along the following lines.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            of the given spacetime represents the viewpoint of a physical observer.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\mu)
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: V \subset X \to \mathbb{R}^4
            be a chart of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            such that there is an open, simply connected subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset \V
            with vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress-energy+tensor"} stress-energy tensor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            Assume that there are two points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in U
            such that the curvature vanishes in a neighborhood of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and does not vanish near
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            describing the situation that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            %span.newWikiWord
              Ricci flat
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Ricci-flat+metric"} ?
            when restricted to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and flat when restricted to a neighborhood of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
          %p
            Given these assumptions, there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi: X \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} X
            (an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi(a) = b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu' = \psi^*(\mu)
            be the pullback of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            are covariant (respect
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            = succeed "s)," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"} pseudo-Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu'
            are solutions to the field equations. So one observer will say that there is no gravitation at the spacetime point
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
          %h2#Discussion Discussion
          %p
            As is often the case when confused about automorphisms, it clarifies the issue to talk about more general
            %em isomorphisms
            first. Thus, consider two different manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            and a diffeomorphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi:Y\cong X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and pulled back metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu' = \psi^*(\mu)
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\mu)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y,\mu')
            represent the same physical reality, with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            as a witness of this fact. In particular, if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi(a)=b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            will coincide with the gravitational field at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Now suppose we have another diffeomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:Y\cong X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu'
            will be different from
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^*(\mu)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            will coincide with the gravitational field at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(a)
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            to be the same and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            to be the identity map.
          %p
            In other words, if we have used some isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            to witness that two manifolds represent the same reality, then we can’t “switch halfway through” our argument and start identifying them along some different isomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is the identity.
          %p
            Notice that this argument has really nothing specifically to do with physics or general relativity. The analogue of what is said here about pseudo-Riemannian manifolds could be said about, say,
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
          %p
            So from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            there is no mystery here, but the above argument originally troubled Einstein, because at his time it was felt that it violates the demand that the statement “at a certain region in time and space, there is (or isn’t) gravitation” should have an objective, observer independent meaning, whether or not there is matter present that “feels” the influence of gravitation. This assumption is based on the Newtonian notions of the absolute, objective existence of space and time. For Newton’s physics, space and time exist independently of any observers and of any objects that are present in time and space. If one adds a structure that models gravitation to space and time in this sense, the statement “at a certain region in time and space, there is (or isn’t) gravitation” is independent of observers and of the presence of further content (or structures) like matter.
          %p
            The conclusion of Einstein and therefore of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            was however that the statement “at a certain region in time and space that contains no matter, there is (or isn’t) gravitation” is
            %em not
            independent of the observer. This means that the
            %em physical
            notions of space and time do not have the same objective meaning as in Newton’s physics.
          %p
            On the other hand, the conclusion that one draws from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            is a simpler and much more general one: when identifing isomorphic objects in a category, we have to remember which isomorphism we used. For a formulation of the situation in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            see the relevant section in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "general+covariance#InHomotopyTypeTheory"} general covariance
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"} general covariance
          %h2#references References
          %p The original discussion:
          %ul
            %li#EinsteinGrossmann
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              , M. Grossmann:
              %em Entwurf einer verallgemeinerten Relativitätstheorie und einer Theorie der Gravitation
              Zeitschrift für Math. Phys. 62, 225–259 (1914)
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sebastian+De+Haro"}> Sebastian De Haro
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremy+Butterfield"} Jeremy Butterfield
              %p
                %em> The Philosophy and Physics of Duality
                , Cambridge University Press (2025) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.01616"}> arXiv:2508.01616
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://global.oup.com/academic/product/the-philosophy-and-physics-of-duality-9780198846338"} ISBN:9780198846338
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (with an eye towards relating to
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+duality"} AdS/CFT duality
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia:
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hole_argument"} Hole argument
          %p
            A treatment in terms of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT"} HoTT
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Dougherty"}> John Dougherty
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Hole Argument, take n
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10701-019-00291-x"} doi
        .revisedby
